WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.020
Vladimir Putin has based his personal
popularity, which is the key to his

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.060
regimes legitimacy, on the propaganda
narrative that looks like this:

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.820
"The motherland is in danger! NATO is about to
attack! But thank God

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.850
President Putin can protect Russia and
restore the glory of the Soviet Union." So

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.140
to sustain this rhetoric, what might he
do next? Shoot down a US plane over

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:30.150
Syria? Crush a possible color revolution
in Belarus? Send Special Ops into Estonia?

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.660
During his meeting with Putin, Trump
should tell him that if Putin

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.679
continues his dangerous policies, the US
will increase the domestic political

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:42.600
cost of his foreign policy. For starters,
the legislation passed by the US Senate

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.120
would squeeze the two sources of his
country's income: oil and arms exports. We

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.929
can send defensive weapons to Ukraine,
dramatically increase our presence on

00:00:51.929 --> 00:00:56.100
the eastern flank of NATO, and can deploy
our own cyber weapons. But if Putin

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.210
begins to moderate his behavior, looking
to internal reforms - not outward

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.410
aggression - for his regime's legitimacy,
the US should be the first one to

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.760
welcome this development.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.100
So what should
President Trump tell President Putin at

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.580
the G20 Summit? Let us know in your
comments. Also, let us know what other

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.170
topics you'd like our scholars to cover
in 60 seconds, and be sure to subscribe

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.780
for more research and videos from AEI.

